  "texts": [
    "How does a pressure cooker cook beans three times faster?",
    "In a normal pot, water boils at 100 degrees.",
    "Turn up the heat all you want, it won't get hotter.",
    "Extra energy just turns more water into steam.",
    "Meanwhile the whole atmosphere presses down on the water.",
    "To boil, steam bubbles must push back against that pressure.",
    "A pressure cooker has a lid with a rubber seal.",
    "Steam can't escape, so the pressure inside builds up.",
    "At double the pressure, water boils only at 120 degrees.",
    "Water 20 degrees hotter cooks food much faster.",
    "Beef that takes two hours is done in 40 minutes.",
    "A valve lets extra steam out to keep it safe.",
    "Never open it under pressure, the water would flash into steam.",
    "On Everest it's the opposite: water boils at just 70 degrees.",
    "That's why climbers carry pressure cookers up mountains.",
    "Follow for more and cook smarter with physics!"
